A core cast trait connects a source type to one builtin target. Choose the
infallible trait when every value of your type can convert. Choose the
fallible trait when the conversion can reject a value.

### Two targets for one type

Suppose a configuration file supplies a port as text. You can always keep
its original spelling, but reading a port number requires a valid `U16`.
Define both methods in the same file as the struct.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    PortSetting = | text String |

    to_string |this PortSetting| -> String {
        return this.text
    }

    try_to_u16 |this PortSetting| -> U16, Error! {
        return cast this.text
    }

    PortSetting must CASTABLE_TO_STRING, TRY_CASTABLE_TO_U16

    setting = PortSetting("443")
    spelling String = cast setting
    port U16 = cast setting catch then 8080
    fallback U16 = cast PortSetting("auto") catch then 8080
]

The first cast returns the text `"443"`. The second calls `try_to_u16`.
Inside that method the declared return type supplies the target for the
builtin cast from String. It succeeds with 443. In the last call the text
can't parse as a number, so the method's `Error!` result reaches the
caller's handler and `fallback` takes the value 8080.

The same source type can provide conversions to different targets. For any
one target, its infallible and fallible traits are mutually exclusive. Moth
uses the complete source and target types to select the method. Knowing
that one particular string contains digits doesn't change that selection.

### Supported targets and signatures

All fifteen targets follow the naming pattern in the example. For an
infallible conversion, append the uppercase target name to `CASTABLE_TO_`
and its lowercase name to `to_`. The method takes only a shared receiver
and returns the target. For a fallible conversion, use `TRY_CASTABLE_TO_`
and `try_to_` and add builtin `Error!` after the success type. Even when
the success type itself is `Error`, the final slot still carries failure.

The numeric targets cover signed (`I`) and unsigned (`U`) integers at
8, 16, 32 and 64 bits and binary floats (`F`) at 16, 32 and 64 bits.
The four other targets are `Bool`, `String`, `Char` and `Error`.
`Byte` and the `Dec` family have builtin conversions only. See
@../casts/#cast-targets (Cast targets) for the supported pairs.

### Conformance and failure

Moth supplies the core trait names. Use them directly in a conformance
declaration or a generic bound. They can't serve as values and you can't
redeclare, import, export, alias or shadow them. A matching method needs
an explicit conformance declaration before `cast` can select it.

A same-file struct or choice can declare conformance. A generic struct or
choice can do so for its whole constructor when the method's type
parameters match the declaration. See @#conformance (Conformance).

An infallible method must handle any operation that could let failure
escape, including inferred failure. A fallible method reports a builtin
`Error`. A custom error needs an explicit mapping to that type. The cast
uses ordinary error handling. Only an ordinary fallible call takes the
postfix propagation marker. Moth resolves cast evidence during type
checking, before lowering. These traits can't introduce new cast targets.
